+---
+id: task-005-json-patch-creation-createpatch
+type: task
+title: "JSON Patch creation (createPatch)"
+status: approved
+format: 1
+---
+
+# Task — JSON Patch creation (createPatch)
+
+## Request
+
+Our clients want to send only what changed between two versions of a JSON document, instead of the whole new
+version. Add a way to produce such a change as a JSON Patch (RFC 6902). Export a function `createPatch(from, to)`
+from `src/index.ts`. It returns a JSON Patch that turns `from` into `to` when applied with `applyPatch`. It must
+not mutate either input. The patch describes what changed and nothing more: when two versions differ in one small
+place, sending the whole document again is not acceptable.
+
+## Scope
+
+- Export `createPatch(from: unknown, to: unknown)` from `src/index.ts`. It returns the operations as an array,
+  typed like the operations `applyPatch` accepts.
+- Use only `add`, `remove` and `replace` operations. No `test`, `move` or `copy`: the patch describes the
+  difference and nothing more.
+- Object members: a member only in `from` is removed, a member only in `to` is added, a member in both is
+  compared recursively.
+- Arrays: the common prefix and the common suffix (by deep equality) are skipped. The middle elements at the
+  same position are compared recursively; surplus elements of `from` are removed and surplus elements of `to`
+  are added. An insertion or a deletion in the middle of an array therefore produces one operation.
+- Any other difference (primitives, or values of different kinds) is one `replace` of the value, and a change of
+  the whole document is a `replace` with path `""`.
+- No patch for equal documents: `createPatch(x, x)` returns `[]`.
+- Values taken from the inputs are copied into the patch, so the patch shares no mutable state with them.
+- Pointers are JSON Pointers (RFC 6901), with `~` and `/` escaped in member names.
+- Inputs that are not JSON values (`undefined`, `NaN`, infinite numbers, functions, `Date`, class instances, sparse
+  arrays, cycles) throw `SyntaxError`, as `applyPatch` and `applyMergePatch` do.
+- Doc comment on the exported function, stating the behaviour and the RFC it implements (directive: documentation),
+  and a section in README.md.
+- Tests under `test/` runnable with `npm test` (`node --test`), red first (directive: testing).
+- No runtime dependency is added (README).
+
+## Acceptance criteria
+
+1. `applyPatch(from, createPatch(from, to))` deep-equals `to`, for every case in the tests.
+2. Equal documents (including equal primitives, `null`, and nested structures) give `[]`.
+3. A single changed member of a nested object gives one `replace` at its pointer; no other operation.
+4. An added member gives one `add`; a removed member gives one `remove`.
+5. An array element changed in place gives one `replace` at its index.
+6. An element inserted or removed in the middle of an array gives one `add` or `remove`, and the rest of the array is
+   not in the patch.
+7. Appended and truncated arrays give `add` and `remove` at the end, with the elements that changed.
+8. A change of kind (object to array, string to number, `null` to object) gives one `replace` at that path; a change
+   of the whole document gives a `replace` with path `""`.
+9. Member names containing `/` or `~` are escaped in the pointer (`~1`, `~0`).
+10. `from` and `to` are not mutated, and values in the patch are not shared with them.
+11. Inputs that are not JSON values throw `SyntaxError`.
+
+## Out of scope
+
+- Minimal patches for reordered arrays or for several scattered changes in one array (move detection, longest
+  common subsequence). The patch stays correct; it may be longer than the minimum.
+- Generating `test` operations or merge patches (RFC 7386).
+- Any change to `applyPatch` or `applyMergePatch`.
